#import "../lib.typ": *

#index-start("Stringere")
Stringere is perhaps one of the most ambiguous concepts in Italian rapier fencing.
Part of this might be due to the lack of good English (or German) translations of the word.
In English one often finds 'gain', or 'find', or 'stringer'.
Some teachers also use 'constrain' which, while not a direct translation, gets at the nature of the action.
I use either 'gain' or the italian 'stringere', although the latter results in somewhat awkward passive constructions.
This document does not try to make sense of how the concept is presented in different sources.#footnote[David Biggs#index[Biggs, David] has written an excellent #link("https://davidbiggs.substack.com/p/so-just-what-is-a-stringere")[blogpost] (#cite(<biggs2025JustWhatStringere>, form: "year")) trying to do just that.]
Instead, I have tried to describe how I think about, and teach, stringere.
The first section describes my notion of stringere---what it is, what it does, and how it does it.
The second section contains the drills I use to teach it.
If I teach the topic on its own, I usually spread it across two classes, but I use many of the same drills when working on other concepts, reviewing the principles of stringere in the process.

=== Principles of Stringere<stringere_principles-of-stringere>

#set quote(block: true)
David #index[Biggs, David] #cite(<biggs2025FindGainStringere>, form: "prose") summarises the technique of stringere as: #quote()[to gain an opponent’s blade, you want to place a greater length of your blade over a lesser length of theirs. And you want to turn your true edge - the edge you would usually try to cut someone with - toward their blade, and aim your point at their silhouette or just past it.]

While there are fine points of stylistic difference (some insist on pointing the true edge toward the ground, for example), this summarises most of the mechanical details.
It does not, however, explain why one would want to perform a stringere in the first place.#footnote[He does so elsewhere, however @biggs2024GainingOpponentsBlade@biggs2024GainingOpponentsBladea@biggs2024GainingOpponentsBladeb@biggs2025FindGainStringere.]

On my view, a stringere does two things: it limits the opponents options and keeps the fencer protected while doing so.
It also puts the fencer in a position to increase pressure on the opponent.



=== Drills<stringere_drills>

These are the drills I use when teaching the topic of stringere.
If I teach the topic on its own, I usually divide it into two classes.
The first class covers drills Ia.--IIb.; the second class reviews drills Ic. and IIa., and covers IIIa--e.

I do each drill for three rounds on each side (inside/outside): first round very slowly, second round at a pace that feels smooth, third round pushing the pace just beyond what is comfortable.
Contrary to other topics, where I will let pairs of students work on a drill for a longer period of time, I have each pair do a drill one time each before rotating to a new partner.

#[
  #set enum(numbering: "Ia.")
  + Basics.
    + A gives a line; B performs a stringere with a gathering step; if A sees an opening for a direct attack, A strikes.
    + As Ia., but B's gathering step must bring them into middle range. If A sees no opening for a direct attack, B strikes with a thrust in opposition.
    + As Ib., but if A sees an opening for a direct attack at any point, they strike.
  + Common reactions to a stringere.
    + A gives a line; B performs a stringere with a gathering step; A performs a disengage (cavazione) and a thrust in opposition.
    + As Ib., when B attacks, A counters _stesso tempo_, with or without a void, with or without an empty-hand parry.
  + Counters to IIa.
    + As IIa.; B counters _a dui tempi_.
    + As IIa.; B counters _stesso tempo_ with a void.
    + As IIa.; B counters _stesso tempo_ with without voiding, with or without an empty-hand parry.
    + As IIa.; B attacks into A's disengage with a thrust in terza (stoccatta).
    + As IIa.; B counters with a counter-disengage (countracavazione) and a thrust in opposition.
]
#index-end("Stringere")
